---
import Base from '../../../layouts/Base.astro';
import '../../../styles/fg04.css';

/*
 * Throwing Shade.
 *
 * Every figure on this page is a 2 m figure computed on 21 July 2026, 06:00 to
 * 20:00 EDT, from lidar flown April to May 2023. Source of record:
 * data/proof/fg04/statistics.json. Nothing here is rounded by hand.
 *
 * Two rules govern the copy, and tests/fg04-content.test.mjs enforces both:
 *   1. This maps shade. It never claims temperature, heat, or coolness.
 *   2. Every neighbourhood and street figure is published twice, measured and
 *      leaf-on corrected, side by side. The aircraft flew in spring, so a
 *      measured figure standing alone reports the month of the flight as a
 *      fact about the city.
 */
---

<Base
  title="Throwing Shade"
  description="Fifteen hourly frames of Toronto's ground shade on 21 July 2026, modelled at 2 m. The shadiest neighbourhoods change once a spring lidar flight is corrected for summer leaves, and the citywide shortage this guide went looking for is not there."
  guideTheme="fg04"
  articlePublished="2026-08-06"
  articleUpdated="2026-08-06"
>
  <div class="fg04">
    <article class="fg04-container">

      <header class="fg04-chapter">
        <span class="fg04-kicker">Shade, towers &amp; trees</span>
        <h1>Throwing Shade</h1>
        <p class="fg04-lede">
          The city thins at one. This maps where the sun reaches the ground in
          Toronto, hour by hour, across a single modelled day.
        </p>
      </header>

      <!-- ============================================================
           Chapter one. Opens on the behaviour, not on the data. No numbers
           until the reader has been told what is being measured and, just as
           importantly, what is not.
           ============================================================ -->
      <section class="fg04-chapter" id="the-map-you-were-already-using">
        <span class="fg04-kicker">Chapter one</span>
        <h2>The map you were already using</h2>

        <p>
          You already do this. Walking a long block in July, you cross to the
          far side without deciding to, then cross back when the buildings run
          out. Nobody taught you the rule. You read the pavement ahead, saw
          where the light stopped, and moved.
        </p>

        <p>
          That habit is a map. You drew it from memory, it covers only the
          streets you walk, and it changes every time a tower goes up or a tree
          comes down. This guide draws the same map for the whole city, on one
          day of one year.
        </p>

        <div class="fg04-scope-note">
          <p>
            <strong>This guide maps shade, not temperature.</strong> Shade is a
            question of what stood between the ground and the sun at a given
            minute. That is all it measures. A shaded asphalt lot can be hotter
            than a sunny lawn, and nothing here measures heat.
          </p>
        </div>

        <p>
          Here is the whole city, averaged across every hour of the modelled
          day. The left figure is the surface the aircraft measured. The right
          figure is the one this guide publishes. Chapter three says why.
        </p>

        <div class="fg04-pair">
          <div class="fg04-pair__half fg04-pair__half--raw">
            <span class="fg04-pair__label">Measured, leaf-off</span>
            <span class="fg04-pair__value">6.201</span>
            <span class="fg04-pair__note">
              mean shaded hours of fifteen, citywide
            </span>
          </div>
          <div class="fg04-pair__half fg04-pair__half--corrected">
            <span class="fg04-pair__label">Leaf-on corrected</span>
            <span class="fg04-pair__value">7.020</span>
            <span class="fg04-pair__note">
              the same ground, with the trees given their summer leaves back
            </span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ============================================================
           Chapter two. The confirmed finding, and the one place the dawn and
           dusk frames get explained. Every later shaded-hour count in the
           guide depends on the reader having been told, once and plainly,
           that the fifteen frames are not fifteen equal hours.
           ============================================================ -->
      <section class="fg04-chapter" id="noon-is-the-cruellest-hour">
        <span class="fg04-kicker">Chapter two</span>
        <h2>Noon is the cruellest hour</h2>

        <p>
          It is not, quite. The sun over Toronto peaks at <strong>13:25
          EDT</strong> on 21 July, not at noon. Daylight saving takes an hour
          of the blame. The rest is Toronto sitting west of the meridian its
          time zone is named for. Shade is thinnest at the modelled hour
          nearest that peak, and that hour is <strong>13:00</strong>.
        </p>

        <div class="fg04-pair">
          <div class="fg04-pair__half fg04-pair__half--raw">
            <span class="fg04-pair__label">Measured, leaf-off</span>
            <span class="fg04-pair__value">10.73%</span>
            <span class="fg04-pair__note">
              of Toronto's ground in shade at 13:00
            </span>
          </div>
          <div class="fg04-pair__half fg04-pair__half--corrected">
            <span class="fg04-pair__label">Leaf-on corrected</span>
            <span class="fg04-pair__value">19.70%</span>
            <span class="fg04-pair__note">
              of Toronto's ground in shade at 13:00, the day's minimum
            </span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p>
          Four fifths of the city stands in open sun at one in the afternoon.
          That is the generous reading. The surface the aircraft measured puts
          it nearer nine tenths.
        </p>

        <h3 class="fg04-kicker">What fifteen frames actually means</h3>

        <p>
          The day runs from <strong>06:00</strong> to <strong>20:00 EDT</strong>
          on <strong>21 July 2026</strong>, sampled once an hour. Fifteen
          frames, computed on a <strong>2 m grid</strong> across the whole
          municipality. Every figure in this guide counts how many of those
          fifteen frames found a patch of ground in shadow.
        </p>

        <p>
          Fifteen frames are not fifteen equal hours. At 06:00 the sun sits
          0.38&deg; above the horizon, at or below the point the model treats
          as sunrise, so that frame finds the city <strong>shaded
          everywhere</strong>. Every patch of ground in Toronto scores one
          shaded hour before anything about it is measured. The 20:00 frame is
          nearly as generous: 84.2% of ground in shade, with the sun under
          8&deg;. So seven shaded hours means seven frames <em>of the modelled
          day</em>, and two of them came free. It does not mean seven hours of
          useful shade, and this guide never says it does.
        </p>

        <p>
          The hours that settle anything are the ones in the middle. That is
          why 13:00 is the frame worth arguing about.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- ============================================================
           Chapter three. The reversal, and the reason the whole correction
           apparatus exists. The claim is narrow on purpose: the order of the
           league table changes, the distances between its top entries do not
           open up, and downtown keeps fourth and fifth. Anything stronger
           than that is not in the data.
           ============================================================ -->
      <section class="fg04-chapter" id="the-month-the-plane-flew">
        <span class="fg04-kicker">Chapter three</span>
        <h2>The month the plane flew</h2>

        <p>
          Ask which part of Toronto sits in the most shade and the measured
          surface answers immediately: downtown. Towers throw long shadows and
          there are a great many towers in a small space. The three shadiest
          neighbourhoods are all tower districts, and the ranking looks
          settled.
        </p>

        <p>
          Correct the same surface for summer leaves and the top of the table
          turns over. All three of the new leaders are leafy midtown
          neighbourhoods, and downtown leaves the top three entirely.
        </p>

        <div class="fg04-ranking">
          <div class="fg04-ranking__col fg04-ranking__col--raw">
            <h4>Measured, leaf-off</h4>
            <ol>
              <li data-moved><span>Yonge-Bay Corridor</span><span class="fg04-ranking__value">10.90</span></li>
              <li data-moved><span>Downtown Yonge East</span><span class="fg04-ranking__value">10.60</span></li>
              <li data-moved><span>Church-Wellesley</span><span class="fg04-ranking__value">10.58</span></li>
              <li><span>Wellington Place</span><span class="fg04-ranking__value">10.24</span></li>
              <li><span>North St. James Town</span><span class="fg04-ranking__value">9.81</span></li>
            </ol>
          </div>
          <div class="fg04-ranking__col fg04-ranking__col--corrected">
            <h4>Leaf-on corrected</h4>
            <ol>
              <li data-moved><span>Rosedale-Moore Park</span><span class="fg04-ranking__value">11.25</span></li>
              <li data-moved><span>Mount Pleasant East</span><span class="fg04-ranking__value">11.17</span></li>
              <li data-moved><span>Lawrence Park South</span><span class="fg04-ranking__value">11.08</span></li>
              <li><span>Church-Wellesley</span><span class="fg04-ranking__value">11.07</span></li>
              <li><span>Yonge-Bay Corridor</span><span class="fg04-ranking__value">11.01</span></li>
            </ol>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p>
          The reason is the aircraft. Every lidar flight ever flown over
          Toronto has been flown in spring, when the ground is visible and the
          survey is cheap to fly. This one flew <strong>April to May
          2023</strong>. A building looks the same in April as it does in July.
          A tree does not. So the measured surface records every tower at its
          full height and every deciduous crown as a bare frame, and the
          ranking it produces is partly a picture of the month the plane flew.
        </p>

        <p>
          That is the whole argument for correcting it, and it is why this
          guide publishes two numbers everywhere instead of one.
        </p>

        <h3 class="fg04-kicker">How big is the reversal, honestly</h3>

        <p>
          Small, and worth saying so. Church-Wellesley and Yonge-Bay Corridor
          are still fourth and fifth once corrected, and the whole corrected
          top five spans <strong>0.24 hours</strong>, about fifteen minutes of
          the modelled day. The order changed. The distances did not open up.
          Downtown did not stop being shady, and on the other end of the table
          it beats Downsview on either surface by a wide margin.
        </p>

        <p>
          The correction is also the generous end of the range. Ground under a
          leaf-on crown is counted as shaded for the whole modelled day, and
          real crowns are not solid: light comes through them, and low sun
          arrives sideways underneath them. The true answer sits somewhere
          between these two columns, nearer the right one in July. That is the
          honest claim, and it is smaller than a headline would like.
        </p>

        <h3 class="fg04-kicker">Towers still work</h3>

        <p>
          The shadiest single arterial in Toronto is <strong>York
          Street</strong>, and it barely moves between the two surfaces.
        </p>

        <div class="fg04-pair">
          <div class="fg04-pair__half fg04-pair__half--raw">
            <span class="fg04-pair__label">Measured, leaf-off</span>
            <span class="fg04-pair__value">11.70</span>
            <span class="fg04-pair__note">
              York Street, mean shaded hours of fifteen
            </span>
          </div>
          <div class="fg04-pair__half fg04-pair__half--corrected">
            <span class="fg04-pair__label">Leaf-on corrected</span>
            <span class="fg04-pair__value">11.74</span>
            <span class="fg04-pair__note">
              York Street again, moved by four hundredths of an hour
            </span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p>
          York Street sits in a trench of towers and almost none of its shade
          comes from trees, so the correction has nothing to add. The point of
          this chapter is not that towers fail to shade a street. They shade it
          reliably, and the aircraft measured them correctly the first time.
          The point is that towers and trees buy shade differently, and a
          spring flight can only see one of them properly.
        </p>
      </section>

    </article>
  </div>
</Base>
